+# 18 · Dark mode and the installable app
+
+> **The ask (15 Oct 2026), translated:** "Run a very heavy layout test on every device, on production, as far as you can. Make sure every screen is responsive and the site works with no bugs, that all the links work, and that the pages and panels that open have no problems. Add a dark/light switcher for the whole system, and add the ability to download it as a mobile app with a simple pop-up button at the bottom-left."
+>
+> **Scope rule (same as Phases 3–5):** do everything that does not need the academy's own facts, money or approval. Nothing here adds a third-party service, a cookie or a dependency.
+>
+> **Delivered in three parts:** *(1)* a light ⇄ dark theme for the whole design system, *(2)* installability as an app (manifest, icons, service worker, and the bottom-left pill), *(3)* the heaviest layout/behaviour sweep this repo has run, plus three new QA suites so the bar stays raised.
+
+---
+
+## 1 · What shipped
+
+| | Before | Now |
+|---|---|---|
+| Themes | One (light) | **Light + dark**, chosen per visitor: the switch is in the header bar **at every width, phones included** (≥ 40 × 40 px, hit-tested), and the mobile sheet carries a second, labelled row so the choice is also visible inside the menu |
+| Theme choice | — | Remembered in `localStorage['ua:theme']`; with no choice, the **OS preference decides**. Applied by an inline script **before the first paint** — no white flash |
+| Dark coverage | — | Every token (surfaces, glass, ink, borders, shadows, gradients, form fields, buttons, chips, focus rings, `::selection`, `color-scheme` for scrollbars and native controls) plus every component that hard-codes a light surface |
+| Installable | No | **Yes**: `manifest.webmanifest`, 192/512/maskable icons, an iOS `apple-touch-icon`, and a service worker — Chrome/Edge/Android offer a real install; iOS Safari gets the Share → Add to Home Screen steps |
+| The install affordance | — | Desktop: a small glass pill, **bottom-inline-start** (bottom-left in English, bottom-right in Arabic). Phones: a row inside the **mobile menu sheet** — the pill is `display: none` under 860 px, so it can never sit on top of the action bar. Both open the same card and appear **only** when the browser offers an install (or on iOS), never inside an already installed app |
+| Offline | — | The worker keeps visited pages, the stylesheet and immutable `/_astro` assets: an installed app opens the last page it knows instead of the browser's error screen |
+| QA suites | 8 | **13**: `theme`, `pwa`, `offline`, `typography` added, plus a pixel contrast audit (`scripts/contrast-audit.mjs`). The canary injects a known bug for each new check, so a green run means something |
+
+## 2 · How the theme is built
+
+1. **One inline script, before any CSS** (`BaseLayout.astro`): reads `ua:theme`, else `prefers-color-scheme`, and writes `data-theme` on `<html>`. Nothing paints before that, so there is no flash of the wrong theme — the usual failure mode of JS-driven dark mode.
+2. **`global.css` carries the dark tokens** (`:root[data-theme='dark']`): background, ink scale, hairlines, accent, focus, glass fills, glass rim and shadows, plus the aurora gradient. The dark theme is the *same* design system, inverted — not a second stylesheet.
+3. **One override block at the end of `global.css`** for everything that hard-codes a light surface (frosted whites, `#fff` fields, the white "verified" pills, the accordion's plus icon drawn as a data URI). It is unlayered so it beats the `components` layer, and it never touches `.theme-night` sections — those are already dark.
+4. **Component-level twins** live in the component's own `<style>` block (header bar, mega panel, language popover, mobile sheet, wizard step bar, tabs, tables…), scoped with `:global([data-theme='dark'])`.
+5. **`src/scripts/theme.ts`** flips the theme, persists it, keeps both toggles' labels/aria in sync (the label always describes the *action*: "Switch to dark theme" / "Switch to light theme"), updates `<meta name="theme-color">` so the Android status bar follows, and fires `theme_switch` for analytics.
+
+## 3 · How the app install works
+
+- `public/manifest.webmanifest` — name, ≤ 12-character `short_name` ("Ummah"), `start_url`, `standalone`, colours, three icons, and three **shortcuts** (free trial, courses, placement test).
+- `public/icons/` — generated by `npm run pwa:icons` (`scripts/build-pwa-icons.mjs`, sharp): the favicon mark on the academy's night gradient, at 192, 512, and a 512 maskable that keeps the mark inside Android's safe zone.
+- `public/sw.js` — HTML **network-first** with the cache as the offline fallback, `/_astro/*` **cache-first** (content-hashed and immutable, so a deploy can never be masked by a stale asset), everything else **stale-while-revalidate**, `/admin/*` **never cached**, old caches dropped on activate, and a bounded cache size. A deploy is picked up on the next navigation.
+- `src/components/InstallPrompt.astro` + `src/scripts/install.ts` — the pill and its card. Chrome: `beforeinstallprompt` is deferred and replayed from our own button. iOS: no event exists, so the pill shows the Share → Add to Home Screen steps instead. "Not now" is remembered; an installed app never sees the pill. `src/scripts/pwa.ts` registers the worker in secure contexts only (so not in `astro dev`, and never on `http://`).
+- `vercel.json` — `/sw.js` is served `must-revalidate` with `Service-Worker-Allowed: /`, and the manifest as `application/manifest+json`.
+
+## 4 · The heavy test (what was actually run)
+
+Everything runs on the **production build** (`dist/`, the same artifact Vercel serves), through a real HTTP server, in real Chrome (headless), with one process per suite. Nothing in this section is "looked at once by a human": each claim is a check that can fail.
+
+| Sweep | Coverage |
+|---|---|
+| `canary` | 11 injected bugs (a too-wide element, a wrapping CTA, an overflowing bar, a mega panel off-screen, tiny tap targets, an iOS-zoom field, unreadable dark-on-dark text, a broken manifest, an uncached page, an image without alt, a paragraph with an orphan last line) — each must be reported |
+| `overflow` | Every page × **10 widths** (320 → 1280 px, including the four unround widths that caught the card bug): nothing leaves the viewport, no text is cut off |
+| `header` | **Every width from 320 to 1440 px** × five languages: no overlap, CTA on one line, actions inside the bar |
+| `popovers` | Mega menu (9 widths), language popover (5 widths), language sheet, mobile `<dialog>`, language hint — **open**, fitting, closable (Escape + outside click), axe-clean |
+| `axe` | WCAG 2.2 AA + best practices on all 194 pages, motion frozen |
+| `cms` | The editor at `/admin/` boots against `config.yml`. Verified here with the **real** pinned bundle (`npm pack @sveltia/cms@0.239.1`, served locally): it reaches its sign-in screen, which also proves the SRI hash matches the published file exactly |
+| `flows` | Booking wizard and placement test, all four tracks, in five languages |
+| `touch` | **10 emulated devices** (iPhone SE portrait *and* landscape, iPhone 15, iPhone 15 Pro Max, Pixel 7, Galaxy S8, iPad mini, Galaxy Tab S8, iPad Air landscape, iPad Pro 12.9 landscape) × key pages × languages: tap targets ≥ 24 px, fields ≥ 16 px, no overflow, mega menu by tap |
+| `theme` | OS preference → theme; the switch flips, persists, survives navigation, relabels; phones get the sheet row (5 languages); **dark-modifier** axe contrast + overflow at 320/390/768/1024/1440 px on ten pages × 5 languages; and the surfaces that only exist while open (mega menu, language popover/sheet, mobile dialog) measured **in dark** |
+| `pwa` | Manifest + icons audited in the page (declared size vs real pixels, maskable presence, shortcut targets), iOS tags and pill markup on every language's home, `sw.js` served as JavaScript with a fetch handler, the pill shown only on offer, inside the viewport, opening its card, remembering "Not now", absent in an installed app |
+| `offline` | Worker registers and controls the page; visited page **and stylesheet** are cached; with the network cut a visited page opens with its own title, an unvisited URL still lands on a real page, `/admin/*` is never answered from the cache; and the network coming back restores normal behaviour |
+
+Extra one-off sweeps (documented, not part of the gate):
+
+- **20 widths** on every page: 320, 360, 375, 390, 414, 430, 480, 540, 600, 720, 768, 820, 900, 1024, 1180, 1280, 1440, 1600, 1920, 2560.
+- Both themes, five languages, RTL included.
